window.__hvBdRegister("plut-lives-5", {"key":"plut-lives-5","sections":[{"i":0,"j":20,"loc":"Life of Demosthenes, chs. 1–11","title":"Demosthenes' origins and training as an orator","summary":"Plutarch opens by arguing that virtue does not depend on a famous native city, explains that he learned Latin late and will compare the two men's lives rather than their oratory, and lists the likenesses of nature and fortune between Demosthenes and Cicero. Demosthenes, son of a wealthy cutler, is cheated by his guardians, kept from exercise as a sickly boy, and nicknamed Batalus. Inspired by hearing Callistratus win the Oropus case, he studies with Isaeus, sues his guardians, and is laughed at in his first speeches to the assembly. Encouraged by Eunomus and the actor Satyrus, he trains his delivery in an underground study, with pebbles in his mouth and before a mirror, and becomes known as an orator who speaks only when prepared.","ideas":["Virtue can grow in any city; the fault for failure lies in ourselves","Demosthenes and Cicero were shaped alike by nature and fortune","Delivery and action count for as much as words in persuasion","Demosthenes' power was won by toil, not by natural gifts"],"terms":[{"t":"bēma (speaker's platform)","d":"The platform in the Athenian assembly from which orators addressed the people."},{"t":"Batalus","d":"A mocking nickname given to the frail young Demosthenes, of disputed meaning."}]},{"i":21,"j":48,"loc":"Life of Demosthenes, chs. 12–22","title":"Demosthenes leads Greece against Philip","summary":"Demosthenes enters public life during the Phocian war and makes resistance to Philip of Macedon his lasting policy; Plutarch defends his consistency against Theopompus but admits he was a poor soldier and open to Persian gold. He drives the Macedonians from Euboea, aids Byzantium and Perinthus, builds a Greek league, and after Philip seizes Elateia wins the Thebans to alliance by his eloquence. Ill omens and oracles precede Chaeroneia, where Demosthenes flees the battle; Philip, sobering after his drunken celebration, fears the orator's power. Athens still honours him with the funeral oration, and when Philip dies he appears garlanded days after his daughter's death, which Plutarch both criticises and defends.","ideas":["Demosthenes held to one policy and finally died for it","His eloquence won Thebes to the alliance against Philip","At Chaeroneia he deserted his post and fled","Public duty as consolation for private grief"],"terms":[{"t":"Philippics","d":"Demosthenes' speeches against Philip of Macedon."},{"t":"Areiopagus","d":"The ancient Athenian council to which Demosthenes brought Antiphon for trial."}]},{"i":49,"j":70,"loc":"Life of Demosthenes, chs. 23–31","title":"Harpalus, exile, and death at Calauria","summary":"After Philip's death Demosthenes stirs revolt against Alexander, but Thebes falls and Alexander demands the surrender of the Athenian orators; Demades buys them a reprieve. Demosthenes wins the trial on the Crown against Aeschines, then accepts a gold cup and twenty talents from the fugitive Harpalus, is condemned by the Areiopagus, and goes into a grieving exile. After Alexander's death he campaigns for the Greek cause, is recalled in honour, but after Crannon he flees from Antipater's agent Archias and takes poison in the temple of Poseidon at Calauria. Plutarch records the versions of his death, the statue and inscription Athens raised to him, and the later execution of Demades.","ideas":["The Harpalus affair shows Demosthenes' weakness for gold","His exile was spent in lament, then in renewed service to Greece","He chose poison rather than surrender to Antipater","Demades the traitor is finally destroyed by those he flattered"],"terms":[{"t":"trial on the Crown","d":"The case against Ctesiphon over a crown proposed for Demosthenes, which Aeschines lost decisively."}]},{"i":71,"j":86,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 1–9","title":"Cicero's youth, studies, and early offices","summary":"Plutarch reports the uncertain origin of Cicero's family and the chick-pea behind his name, portents at his birth, and his brilliance as a schoolboy and poet. He studies with Philon the Academic and Scaevola, defends Roscius against Sulla's freedman Chrysogonus, then goes to Greece and Asia, studying with Antiochus, Apollonius and Poseidonius; Apollonius laments that Rome now takes Greece's eloquence. Back in Rome he becomes a leading advocate, serves justly as quaestor in Sicily, learns from a deflating encounter how little his fame has spread, and prosecutes Verres without fees. As praetor he is a strict judge and ends by agreeing to defend Manilius for Pompey's sake.","ideas":["Cicero prized philosophy and long considered a quiet life in Athens","The Delphic oracle told him to follow his own nature, not popular opinion","His love of glory remained excessive all his life","A statesman should know the citizens who are his instruments"],"terms":[{"t":"New Academy","d":"The sceptical school of Carneades and Philon, which Cicero preferred to Antiochus' Stoicising innovations."}]},{"i":87,"j":106,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 10–22","title":"Cicero's consulship and the Catilinarian conspiracy","summary":"With Rome unsettled and Pompey abroad, Catiline gathers ruined nobles and Sulla's veterans, and Cicero is elected consul with Antonius, whom he wins over with the province of Macedonia. Cicero defeats the land commission, calms a theatre riot, and after Crassus brings him anonymous letters obtains emergency powers from the senate; he survives an assassination plot and drives Catiline from Rome. Lentulus and Cethegus plan to burn the city, but letters given to the Allobroges are seized and the conspirators are exposed in the senate. After a sign at the Bona Dea rites and a debate in which Caesar urges imprisonment and Cato demands death, Cicero has the men executed and is escorted home as saviour of his country.","ideas":["Rome's debts and Sulla's legacy left the state ripe for revolution","Cicero's eloquence shows justice is invincible when well spoken","Caesar argues for mercy; Cato for death","Cicero quells the conspiracy with few evils and no civil strife"],"terms":[{"t":"Bona Dea","d":"A goddess whose women-only rites were held in the consul's house."},{"t":"\"They have lived\"","d":"The Roman way of announcing death without words of ill omen."}]},{"i":107,"j":115,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 23–27","title":"Father of his country, self-praise, and jests","summary":"Caesar and the tribunes Metellus and Bestia try to silence Cicero as he leaves office, but he swears that he saved his country and Cato has him named father of his country. Plutarch then turns to his character: his endless self-praise made him odious, though he was generous in praising others, calling Aristotle a river of gold and recommending Cratippus. A long series of his sharp sayings follows, aimed at Crassus, Vatinius, Metellus Nepos and others. Plutarch judges that such jests against opponents suit an orator, but that his indiscriminate wit for the sake of a laugh earned him many enemies.","ideas":["Cicero was the first called father of his country","Constant boasting made his charm tedious","He was ungrudging in praise of others","Biting wit made him widely hated"],"terms":[{"t":"pater patriae (father of his country)","d":"The title given to Cicero after Cato proclaimed it before the people."}]},{"i":116,"j":125,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 28–35","title":"Clodius, exile, return, and Milo's trial","summary":"Clodius is caught in disguise at the women's rites in Caesar's house; Cicero, urged on by Terentia, testifies against him, but bribed jurors acquit him. As tribune Clodius turns on Cicero over the execution of the conspirators; Caesar is alienated, Pompey avoids him, and Cicero leaves Rome and is banished. He spends his exile in dejection, while Clodius burns his house and builds a temple of Liberty on its site. Pompey and Milo bring about his triumphant return in the sixteenth month; Cicero later destroys Clodius' records, quarrels quietly with Cato, and, frightened by Pompey's soldiers, pleads poorly for Milo after Clodius is killed.","ideas":["The Bona Dea scandal turns Clodius into Cicero's enemy","Pompey betrays Cicero to oblige Caesar","Public opinion can wash reason from a statesman's soul","Cicero lacked courage both under arms and in speaking"],"terms":[{"t":"fire and water","d":"Refusal of fire and water was the Roman form of outlawry imposed on Cicero."}]},{"i":126,"j":133,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 36–40","title":"Cilicia, civil war, and Caesar's favour","summary":"Cicero becomes an augur and governs Cilicia mildly and honestly, refusing gifts and winning a small victory over mountain robbers. Returning as civil war breaks out, he tries to reconcile Caesar and Pompey, hesitates, and finally joins Pompey, where Cato rebukes him and his jokes mock the camp. After Pharsalus he refuses Cato's offer of command, is nearly killed by the young Pompey, and waits at Brundisium, where Caesar greets him with honour. His speech for Ligarius moves Caesar to acquit; under the monarchy he withdraws to write philosophy, coining Latin terms for Greek concepts.","ideas":["Cicero governs his province without greed or cruelty","He knew whom to flee but not to whom to flee","Caesar treats him with honour after Pharsalus","He creates a Latin vocabulary for Greek philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"Imperator","d":"The title his soldiers gave Cicero after his victory on Mount Amanus."},{"t":"katalēpsis, epochē","d":"Greek philosophical terms (comprehension, suspension of judgement) for which Cicero supplied Latin names."}]},{"i":134,"j":146,"loc":"Life of Cicero, chs. 41–49","title":"Antony, Octavian, proscription, and Cicero's murder","summary":"Cicero divorces Terentia, remarries a rich young ward, and grieves bitterly at Tullia's death. He takes no part in Caesar's assassination but proposes an amnesty, then is driven into conflict with Antony and allies with the young Caesar, whose rule he had seen in a dream. At the height of his power he helps Caesar to the consulship, only to be abandoned when Caesar, Antony and Lepidus form their alliance and give him up to proscription. After a wavering flight he is overtaken in his litter near Caieta and killed by Herennius; Antony has his head and hands set on the rostra, and later the young Caesar praises him and makes his son consul.","ideas":["An old man is outwitted by a young one","No wild beast is more savage than man with power","Cicero dies facing his killers calmly","The family of Cicero shares in Antony's final punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"proscription","d":"A published list of men to be killed, here drawn up by Caesar, Antony and Lepidus."}]},{"i":147,"j":156,"loc":"Comparison of Demosthenes and Cicero, chs. 1–5","title":"Comparison of Demosthenes and Cicero","summary":"Plutarch contrasts Demosthenes' concentrated, serious eloquence with Cicero's broad learning and fondness for jest, and condemns Cicero's boasting as a craving for fame. He notes that Cicero, unlike Demosthenes, held real power and showed in office contempt for wealth and humanity, fulfilling Plato's hope of power joined to wisdom and justice, while Demosthenes was tainted by Persian and Harpalan money. Demosthenes' exile was disgraceful in cause but useful to Greece, Cicero's honourable in cause but idle. Cicero's death as a fleeing old man is pitiable, while Demosthenes' poison is admired as an escape from Antipater's cruelty.","ideas":["Demosthenes devoted all his powers to rhetoric; Cicero to wide learning","Power and office test a man's character","Cicero refused gifts; Demosthenes did not","The two exiles and deaths are judged in opposite ways"],"terms":[]},{"i":157,"j":166,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 1–7","title":"Bad lives as examples; Demetrius' youth and first wars","summary":"Plutarch argues that, as arts study opposites, his readers may learn from the lives of bad men too, and announces Demetrius and Antony as great natures with great vices. Demetrius, son of Antigonus, is strikingly handsome, pleasure-loving in peace but energetic in war, devoted to his father, and humane enough to warn his friend Mithridates of danger. At twenty-two he is badly beaten by Ptolemy at Gaza, receives his belongings back, and repays the kindness after defeating Ptolemy's general Cilles. He then raids the Nabataean Arabs and Babylonia and relieves Halicarnassus.","ideas":["Knowledge of bad lives helps us choose the better","Great natures show great vices as well as virtues","Demetrius models himself on Dionysus","Defeat at Gaza and the exchange of courtesies with Ptolemy"],"terms":[]},{"i":167,"j":182,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 8–14","title":"Liberation of Athens and its extravagant honours","summary":"Antigonus and Demetrius decide to free Greece, and Demetrius sails into Piraeus, proclaims the freedom of Athens, sends Demetrius the Phalerean safely to Thebes, and frees Megara, narrowly escaping capture while visiting Cratesipolis. He restores the democracy, but the Athenians, led by Stratocles, heap absurd honours on him: the title of king, Saviour-gods, renamed months, and even consulting him as an oracle. Plutarch records omens of divine displeasure and Philippides' verses against Stratocles. The section ends with Demetrius' many marriages, including Phila, and his notorious courtesans.","ideas":["The goodwill of a people is the surest gangway to Greece","Flattery corrupts Demetrius' mind","Divine signs mark the impious honours","Demetrius treats marriage lightly"],"terms":[{"t":"Sōtēres (Saviour-gods)","d":"The divine title the Athenians gave Antigonus and Demetrius."}]},{"i":183,"j":195,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 15–22","title":"Victory at Cyprus, kingship, and the siege of Rhodes","summary":"Demetrius defeats Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in Cyprus, captures the courtesan Lamia, and is generous to the defeated; Aristodemus' theatrical delivery of the news prompts the army to hail Antigonus and Demetrius as kings, and the other successors follow. Plutarch notes how the royal title made the kings more pompous and harsh. After a failed invasion of Egypt, Plutarch describes Demetrius' alternating pleasure and energy and his genius for building great ships and siege engines. He besieges Rhodes with the giant City-taker, spares Protogenes' painting of Ialysus, and finally makes terms.","ideas":["A flatterer's word changes the conduct of kings","Demetrius keeps pleasure and war wholly separate","His engines impress even his enemies","He would rather burn his father's portraits than a masterpiece"],"terms":[{"t":"helepolis (City-taker)","d":"Demetrius' enormous siege tower, many storeys high, used against Rhodes."}]},{"i":196,"j":206,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 23–27","title":"Debauchery in Athens and the Peloponnese","summary":"Demetrius drives Cassander from Attica and is lodged in the Parthenon, where he defiles the acropolis with courtesans; the boy Democles kills himself to escape him, and Athens votes that whatever Demetrius ordains is just. In the Peloponnese he frees cities, marries Deïdameia, renames Sicyon, and is proclaimed commander of the Greeks, while mocking the other kings' titles. He forces the Athenians to rearrange the calendar so he can be initiated into all grades of the Mysteries at once. Plutarch ends with anecdotes of Lamia's power over him and her extortions.","ideas":["The Athenians mistake removal of a garrison for freedom","Democles' death shows a spirit worthy of his city","Sacred custom is bent for Demetrius' initiation","Lamia's sway over Demetrius breeds envy"],"terms":[{"t":"epoptika","d":"The highest grade of the Eleusinian Mysteries, normally reached only after a year's interval."}]},{"i":207,"j":219,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 28–34","title":"Ipsus, Athens' rejection, and the siege of Athens","summary":"The other kings combine against Antigonus, who becomes unusually grave and names Demetrius his successor; ill omens precede the battle. Demetrius routs Antiochus but pursues too far, and Antigonus is killed while waiting for his son. Refused entry by Athens, Demetrius reflects bitterly on honours given from fear, then raids Lysimachus' lands, marries his daughter Stratonicé to Seleucus, and falls out with Seleucus over Cilicia, Tyre and Sidon. He returns to Greece, besieges Athens under the tyrant Lachares, and after a terrible famine enters the city, reproves the people mildly, and garrisons it.","ideas":["Antigonus' harshness united the young kings against him","Rash pursuit throws away the victory at Ipsus","Honours given from fear are worthless","Insatiable greed keeps even Seleucus poor"],"terms":[]},{"i":220,"j":231,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 35–40","title":"King of Macedonia; Antiochus and Stratonicé","summary":"Demetrius beats the Spartans near their city but is called away by losses in Asia and Cyprus, as Fortune turns again. Summoned into Cassander's quarrelling family, he outwits the young Alexander at Larissa, has him killed, and is proclaimed king of Macedonia. Plutarch tells how the physician Erasistratus discovered that Antiochus was dying of love for his stepmother Stratonicé, and how Seleucus gave her to his son. Demetrius takes Thessaly, twice besieges and captures Thebes, showing clemency, and holds the Pythian games in Athens.","ideas":["Fortune raises and lowers Demetrius more than any other king","He seizes Macedonia by forestalling a plot","Erasistratus reads love in the body's signs","Demetrius wastes his soldiers' lives at Thebes"],"terms":[]},{"i":232,"j":248,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 41–45","title":"Theatrical kingship and the loss of Macedonia","summary":"Pyrrhus' victory over Demetrius' general makes the Macedonians compare him to Alexander, while Demetrius' gold-shot robes and haughty inaccessibility offend them; an old woman tells him not to be king if he has no time. Plutarch reflects that justice, not city-taking, befits a king. Demetrius prepares a vast army and fleet to reconquer Asia, but Seleucus, Ptolemy and Lysimachus ally with Pyrrhus against him. His army deserts to Pyrrhus, he steals away in disguise, and Phila poisons herself; Plutarch quotes Sophocles' image of fortune as the changing moon.","ideas":["Demetrius plays king like an actor on a stage","Justice is the proper work of a king","His soldiers desert to Pyrrhus without a battle","Fortune waxes and wanes like the moon"],"terms":[{"t":"Poliorcetes (City-besieger)","d":"Demetrius' surname, which Plutarch contrasts with Zeus' titles as city-guardian."}]},{"i":249,"j":269,"loc":"Life of Demetrius, chs. 45–53","title":"Last campaigns, captivity, and death of Demetrius","summary":"Demetrius recovers some strength in Greece, lifts the siege of Athens at Crates' persuasion, marries Ptolemaïs, and invades Asia against Lysimachus, but is worn down by Agathocles, famine and sickness. Pressed into Seleucus' territory, he fights well until illness and the desertion of his mercenaries to Seleucus leave him trapped; he surrenders. Seleucus, swayed by jealous courtiers, confines him in the Syrian Chersonese, where he gives himself to drink and dice and dies after three years. Antigonus escorts his ashes in solemn procession to Demetrias, Plutarch lists his children, and turns to Antony's Life.","ideas":["Seleucus' generosity turns to suspicion","Demetrius tells his son to treat him as dead","In captivity he finds his good in idleness and drink","His funeral is as theatrical as his life"],"terms":[]},{"i":270,"j":285,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 1–10","title":"Antony's family, youth, and rise under Caesar","summary":"Plutarch opens with Antony's generous father and his mother Julia, then tells how friendship with Curio led the young Antony into debt and dissipation before he left for Greece to train in arms and Asiatic oratory. Serving under Gabinius, he storms Aristobulus' forts, takes Pelusium, and wins a name for daring and humanity. Plutarch sketches his Heraclean looks, soldierly familiarity, and lavish giving. As tribune Antony champions Caesar in the senate and flees to him in a slave's dress; Plutarch rejects Cicero's charge that he caused the civil war. He serves bravely in the Brundisium crossing and at Pharsalus, but as Master of Horse in Rome his drinking, mimes, and luxury make him hated, until marriage to the domineering Fulvia tames him somewhat.","ideas":["Curio's corrupting friendship and Antony's early debts","Military brilliance in Syria and Egypt and before Pharsalus","Popularity with soldiers against odium among citizens for his excesses","Fulvia schools Antony to obey a woman, preparing the way for Cleopatra"],"terms":[{"t":"Asiatic style","d":"A florid, boastful manner of oratory that Plutarch says matched Antony's own life."},{"t":"Master of Horse (magister equitum)","d":"The dictator's deputy, who held almost sole authority in Rome while the dictator was absent."}]},{"i":286,"j":299,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 11–22","title":"From the Lupercalia to Philippi","summary":"Caesar honours Antony and makes him consular colleague; at the Lupercalia Antony twice offers Caesar a diadem, giving the conspirators their pretext. Brutus forbids killing Antony, who after the Ides first brokers an amnesty, then turns the crowd against the assassins with his funeral speech and Caesar's bloodied robe. Seizing Caesar's papers and treasure, he slights the young Caesar (Octavius), is declared a public enemy at Cicero's urging, and is beaten at Mutina, enduring famine with admirable hardihood before winning over Lepidus' army. The triumvirs then divide the empire and trade victims, Cicero for Lucius Caesar, which Plutarch calls the most savage of exchanges. At Philippi Antony defeats Cassius and gains the credit for the final victory over Brutus, whose body he honours.","ideas":["The offer of the diadem at the Lupercalia","Antony's funeral oration stirs the people against the assassins","Antony at his best in adversity during the retreat from Mutina","The proscriptions and the exultation over Cicero's head and hand"],"terms":[{"t":"Charonitae","d":"Mocking Roman name for men appointed or recalled on the pretext of the dead Caesar's memoranda."},{"t":"triumvirate","d":"The joint rule of Antony, Caesar (Octavius), and Lepidus, agreed on a river island and hated for its exactions."}]},{"i":300,"j":313,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 23–29","title":"Antony in the East meets Cleopatra","summary":"Antony courts the Greeks as a Philhellene, then moves into Asia, where kings and flatterers crowd his quarters and Ephesus hails him as Dionysus while he strips the wealthy of their property; Hybreas' bold speech shows him his ignorance of his agents' extortions. Plutarch analyses his simple, trusting nature and his weakness for flattery mixed with frankness. Summoned to Cilicia, Cleopatra sails up the Cydnus in a gilded barge dressed as Venus and outdoes Antony in feasting; her charm, Plutarch says, lay less in beauty than in conversation and her many languages. She carries him off to Alexandria, where the Inimitable Livers feast without end, as the physician Philotas' story of eight roasting boars shows, and she keeps him in constant play, as in the salted-fish prank.","ideas":["Antony hailed as Dionysus at Ephesus amid extortion","Frank jesting used as a sauce for flattery","Cleopatra's arrival on the Cydnus","The extravagance of the Inimitable Livers at Alexandria"],"terms":[{"t":"Inimitable Livers","d":"The society of Antony and Cleopatra's companions who feasted one another daily at incredible cost."},{"t":"Philhellene","d":"Lover of the Greeks, a title Antony enjoyed while in Greece."}]},{"i":314,"j":324,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 30–37","title":"Octavia, uneasy truces, and Cleopatra again","summary":"Roused by news of Fulvia's war in Italy and the Parthian invasion under Labienus, Antony sails west; Fulvia dies and he is reconciled with Caesar, the empire being divided at the Ionian sea, and marries Caesar's widowed sister Octavia. At Misenum Sextus Pompeius refuses Menas' offer to cut the cables and seize the triumvirs, since perjury is not his way. An Egyptian seer warns Antony that his genius is cowed by Caesar's, as their games of chance confirm. Ventidius defeats the Parthians and triumphs, while Antony bungles the siege of Samosata. Octavia reconciles the two men at Tarentum, but in Syria Antony's passion for Cleopatra flares again; he grants her wide territories, acknowledges their twins, and launches the Parthian war with a vast army, hurried on by longing for her.","ideas":["Marriage to Octavia meant to secure peace between Antony and Caesar","Sextus Pompeius declines treachery at Misenum","Antony's genius said to be overawed by Caesar's","Cleopatra's renewed hold, likened to Plato's unruly horse of the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (guardian genius)","d":"A person's attendant spirit; the seer claims Antony's quails before Caesar's."}]},{"i":325,"j":335,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 38–44","title":"The Parthian campaign: Phraata and retreat","summary":"Impatient to return to Cleopatra, Antony leaves his siege engines behind under Statianus, who is cut off and killed with ten thousand men; Artavasdes of Armenia then deserts. Antony's battle with the Parthians yields few enemy dead, and he decimates the cowards who abandon the siege mound. Deceived by Parthian assurances of safe passage, he withdraws, but a Mardian guide warns him to keep to the hills and detects a breached dike set to impede the march. In the fighting that follows Flavius Gallus pushes too far and Canidius' piecemeal relief costs three thousand dead. Antony weeps among the wounded, who still call him Imperator, and Plutarch praises the devotion of his soldiers; Antony prays that any retribution fall on him alone.","ideas":["Loss of the siege train and the treachery of Artavasdes","Decimation of the troops who fled the mound","Parthian feigned friendship and the loyal Mardian guide","The soldiers' extraordinary devotion to Antony"],"terms":[{"t":"decimation","d":"Punishment in which one man in every ten, chosen by lot, is put to death."}]},{"i":336,"j":343,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 45–50","title":"Famine, thirst, and the march to Armenia","summary":"The Romans repel Parthian attacks with the shield-roof, but famine forces them to eat unfamiliar plants, one of which drives men to dig at stones and die; Antony cries out in admiration of Xenophon's Ten Thousand. Mithridates, cousin of Monaeses, warns that an ambush awaits on the plains, so Antony takes the waterless mountain road by night. Men drink from a poisonous salt river, and in a night of plunder within the army Antony makes his freedman swear to kill him if needed. At dawn they reach a drinkable river, which the Parthians let them cross, and six days later the Araxes and Armenia, where they weep for joy. A review shows twenty thousand foot and four thousand horse lost, more to disease than battle; Plutarch blames Artavasdes, whom Antony later seizes.","ideas":["The shield-roof that turns aside Parthian arrows","The madness-inducing herb and the comparison with the Ten Thousand","Mithridates' warning and the night march through the mountains","Eighteen victories that were never decisive"],"terms":[{"t":"Ten Thousand","d":"Xenophon's Greek army that marched from Babylon to the sea, which Antony invokes in admiration."}]},{"i":344,"j":353,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 51–59","title":"Octavia spurned and the Donations of Alexandria","summary":"After losing eight thousand more men to snow, Antony waits drunkenly at White Village for Cleopatra. Octavia sails out with supplies and troops, but Antony bids her stay at Athens, while Cleopatra feigns pining and her flatterers persuade him to return to Alexandria. Octavia keeps his house in Rome and raises all his children, which makes Antony more hated. In the gymnasium at Alexandria he proclaims Cleopatra queen with Caesarion and his sons Kings of Kings, and she appears as the New Isis. Charges are traded with Caesar; Antony gathers his fleet, takes Cleopatra to Samos for festivals, and divorces Octavia. Deserters reveal his will, which Caesar seizes from the Vestals and reads to the senate, and Cleopatra drives off friends like Geminius who urge her return to Egypt.","ideas":["Cleopatra's feigned lovesickness defeats Octavia's dignity","The theatrical distribution of kingdoms to Cleopatra's children","Festivities at Samos while the world prepares for war","Caesar's reading of Antony's will to the senate"],"terms":[{"t":"Kings of Kings","d":"Title Antony proclaimed for his sons by Cleopatra, with eastern realms allotted to each."},{"t":"New Isis","d":"Name under which Cleopatra appeared in public wearing the goddess's sacred robe."}]},{"i":354,"j":368,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 60–68","title":"War declared and the battle of Actium","summary":"Rome declares war on Cleopatra, Caesar claiming that Antony is drugged and that her eunuch and maids govern. Omens strike Antony's Heracles and Dionysus statues and Cleopatra's flagship. Though stronger on land, Antony chooses a naval battle to please Cleopatra, with undermanned ships; Domitius and allied kings desert, and Canidius urges a land fight in vain. A scarred centurion begs to fight on land. At Actium the heavy ships fight like a siege until Cleopatra's sixty ships flee and Antony abandons his men to follow her, sitting three days alone at her prow. His fleet resists until the tenth hour and his legions hold out seven days before going over. Plutarch adds his great-grandfather's tale of his fellow citizens freed from carrying grain for Antony.","ideas":["Choosing the sea to please Cleopatra despite land superiority","The centurion's plea to fight on land","Cleopatra's flight and Antony's desertion of his fleet","The soldiers' loyalty after their commander had fled"],"terms":[{"t":"Actium","d":"The gulf off Epirus where the decisive sea-fight was fought and where Nicopolis later stood."}]},{"i":369,"j":387,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 69–79","title":"Last days in Egypt and Antony's death","summary":"Antony wanders in Libya, is stopped from suicide, and builds the Timoneum at Pharos, imitating the misanthrope Timon, whose sayings and epitaphs Plutarch records. Learning that his forces and allies are gone, he returns to revels, founding the Partners in Death, while Cleopatra tests poisons and finds the asp gentlest. Caesar rejects their embassy; Antony flogs the envoy Thyrsus. After a final cavalry success, Antony hears in the night a Bacchic throng leaving the city, read as Dionysus deserting him. His fleet and horse go over to Caesar; told falsely that Cleopatra is dead, he stabs himself after Eros kills himself, is hauled up into her tomb, and dies urging her to trust Proculeius. Caesar weeps, and Proculeius takes Cleopatra alive.","ideas":["Antony as a second Timon the misanthrope","From the Inimitable Livers to the Partners in Death","The god's procession abandoning Antony","Antony's death in Cleopatra's arms, conquered as a Roman by a Roman"],"terms":[{"t":"Timoneum","d":"Antony's retreat built on a mole at Pharos, named for Timon of Athens."},{"t":"Partners in Death","d":"The society Antony and Cleopatra founded for friends resolved to die together."}]},{"i":388,"j":399,"loc":"Life of Antony, chs. 80–87","title":"Cleopatra's death and Antony's descendants","summary":"Caesar enters Alexandria and pardons the city for the sake of Alexander, its beauty, and the philosopher Areius. Antyllus and later Caesarion are killed. Cleopatra buries Antony, tries to starve herself, and is coerced through threats to her children; she receives Caesar dishevelled, beats a steward who exposes her hidden treasure, and convinces Caesar she wishes to live. Warned by Dolabella that she is to be sent away, she pours a last libation at Antony's urn, bathes, dines, and dies with Iras and Charmion, perhaps by an asp in a basket of figs, though Plutarch reports the uncertainty. Caesar buries her with Antony. Plutarch ends with Antony's seven children, reared by Octavia, and his line down to Nero.","ideas":["Caesar's mercy to Alexandria at Areius' request","Cleopatra deceives Caesar into thinking she wants to live","The lament at the urn and the basket of figs","Charmion's last words and the uncertain manner of death"],"terms":[{"t":"asp","d":"The venomous snake Cleopatra had found to give the gentlest death; accounts of its use differ."}]},{"i":400,"j":411,"loc":"Comparison of Demetrius and Antony, chs. 1–6","title":"Comparison of Demetrius and Antony","summary":"Plutarch weighs the two men who suffered great reversals. Antony rose by his own efforts while Demetrius inherited power, but Antony's aim was tyrannical, enslaving a Rome just freed, whereas Demetrius freed Greek cities. Demetrius was more generous and never let pleasure spoil action, while Antony, like Heracles disarmed by Omphale, was unmanned by Cleopatra and fled Actium like Paris. Antony's double marriage broke Roman law, yet Demetrius' debauchery profaned the Parthenon and involved cruelty. Antony's surrender of his uncle was abominable; Demetrius won his own victories, Antony through generals. Antony deserted his men while Demetrius was deserted; and Antony's death, though ignoble, came before capture, while Demetrius endured captivity.","ideas":["Self-made power against inherited power","Antony's aim judged tyrannical, Demetrius' blameless","Antony disarmed by Cleopatra like Heracles by Omphale","Neither death commendable, but Demetrius' more blameworthy"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"Plutarch's formal weighing of the paired Lives' virtues and faults."}]},{"i":412,"j":424,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 1–10","title":"Dion, Plato, and the court of Dionysius","summary":"Addressing Sossius Senecio, Plutarch pairs Dion with Brutus as men trained in Plato's doctrine and both warned of death by a spectre. He recounts the elder Dionysius' two wives, Doris and Aristomache, Dion's sister. Dion, favoured by the tyrant, is fired by Plato's teaching and arranges a meeting at which Plato argues that tyrants lack courage and the unjust are wretched; the angry tyrant has Plato sold at Aegina. Dion keeps his influence, rebukes Dionysius over Gelon, and after the tyrant's death impresses the young Dionysius by offering ships for war. Courtiers corrupt the new ruler with pleasure and slander Dion's austerity. Dion urges him to study with Plato and rule by goodwill, not fear.","ideas":["Dion and Brutus as students of one school, linked by spectres","Plato sold into slavery after angering the elder Dionysius","Dion's grave and harsh manner versus a pleasure-loving court","True 'adamantine bonds' of rule are goodwill and justice"],"terms":[{"t":"adamantine bonds","d":"The elder Dionysius' phrase for what held his tyranny; Dion argues they are virtue and goodwill, not force."},{"t":"authadeia (self-will)","d":"The harsh stubbornness Plato warned Dion against as a companion of solitude."}]},{"i":425,"j":435,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 11–17","title":"Plato's second visit and Dion's exile","summary":"Persuaded by Dion and the Pythagoreans, Plato comes to Syracuse lest he seem all theory, and Dion's enemies recall the historian Philistus as a counterweight. Plato is received with honour, the court turns to geometry, and Dionysius even rebukes the herald praying for the tyranny's permanence. The courtiers accuse Dion of bewitching Dionysius so his nephews might rule; an intercepted letter to Carthage lets Dionysius put Dion on a boat to Italy. Dionysius keeps Plato in the acropolis with a jealous passion for his love, then sends him home, promising Dion's recall. Dion lives at the Academy, is softened by Speusippus' company, and wins honours across Greece, including Spartan citizenship.","ideas":["Plato's motive: to cure Sicily by reforming one ruler","Philistus recalled as the champion of tyranny","Dion's exile over the Carthaginian letter","Dionysius' jealous, tyrannical attachment to Plato"],"terms":[{"t":"Academy","d":"Plato's school at Athens, where Dion studied in exile and which he later regarded as his true audience."}]},{"i":436,"j":443,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 18–22","title":"Plato's third visit and the break","summary":"Jealous of Dion's fame, Dionysius stops his revenues but longs again for Plato, and through Archytas and the Pythagoreans, with threats and promises about Dion, brings him back for a third voyage. Plato refuses gifts and pleads for Dion, but Dionysius sells Dion's estate and puts Plato among hostile mercenaries until Archytas secures his release; Plato quips that the Academy has better topics than Dionysius. Dionysius then forces Dion's wife Arete to marry Timocrates, unlike his father, whose sister Theste is remembered for her bold answer. Dion resolves on war; Plato holds aloof, but Speusippus reports that Syracuse longs for him, and Dion assembles fewer than eight hundred picked mercenaries at Zacynthus.","ideas":["Plato's third crossing 'to the straits of Scylla'","The seizure of Dion's property and Plato's danger","Arete given to Timocrates against her will","Dion's small, elite force gathered with Academy support"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagoreans","d":"Archytas and his circle in Italy, who stood surety for Plato's safety."}]},{"i":444,"j":453,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 23–29","title":"The expedition and the liberation of Syracuse","summary":"Dion's mercenaries balk until he and Alcimenes convince them they will lead a ready revolt. After a sacrifice and banquet the moon is eclipsed; Dion understands its cause, and the seer Miltas reads it as the eclipse of the tyranny, though privately he fears the omen of the bees. Storms drive the ships to Africa before they land at Minoa, held by Dion's friend Synalus. With Dionysius away in Italy and his warning letter lost to a wolf, Dion marches on Syracuse, gathering thousands, and enters in procession to welcome after forty-eight years of tyranny. From the tyrant's sun-dial he proclaims liberty, is made general with full powers, and walls off the acropolis before Dionysius returns.","ideas":["The lunar eclipse explained and reinterpreted","Storm-driven voyage by way of Africa","The wolf and the lost dispatch","Triumphal entry and liberty proclaimed from the sun-dial"],"terms":[{"t":"Epipolae","d":"The heights of Syracuse, abandoned by its garrison and seized by Dion."},{"t":"Achradina","d":"A quarter of Syracuse through which Dion passed to address the people."}]},{"i":454,"j":463,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 30–37","title":"Treachery, suspicion, and Heracleides' rivalry","summary":"Dionysius feigns negotiations, then sends drunken mercenaries against the siege-wall; Dion, though old, fights in front, is wounded, and rallies the city to victory. A letter written in his son's name urging him to take the tyranny makes the Syracusans suspect him, and they turn to the exile Heracleides, whom Dion magnanimously gets appointed admiral, though he intrigues in secret. Sosis fakes wounds to accuse Dion's mercenaries and is exposed by the razor and condemned. The citizens defeat Philistus at sea and abuse his body; Plutarch criticises both Timaeus' slanders and Ephorus' praise of him. Dionysius offers to surrender, is refused, and slips away by sea, leaving his son in the citadel.","ideas":["Dion's personal courage in the night assault","The letter designed to make Dion suspect","The demagogue Heracleides courts the crowd","Plutarch's verdict on the historians' treatment of Philistus"],"terms":[{"t":"demagogue","d":"A leader who flatters the populace; Heracleides is the type, against Dion's gravity."}]},{"i":464,"j":477,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 37–47","title":"Dion expelled, recalled, and Syracuse saved","summary":"Heracleides backs Hippo's land redistribution and the people vote to stop the mercenaries' pay and replace Dion, despite thunder and a runaway ox. Dion's men escort him out; he refuses to fight his fellow citizens and goes to Leontini, where the allies judge Syracuse in the wrong. The tyrant's officer Nypsius then sacks the drunken, careless city. Shamed, the Syracusans summon Dion; with tears he asks his mercenaries to help, and they agree. When the demagogues rebuff him, Nypsius burns and slaughters, and Dion races in through fire and ruins to rout the enemy. Heracleides and Theodotes surrender, and Dion pardons them, saying the Academy taught him to master anger.","ideas":["The people's untimely bid for independence after long tyranny","Dion's refusal to shed citizens' blood","The two sacks of Syracuse by Nypsius","Forgiveness of Heracleides as a test of philosophic virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"gēs anadasmos (redistribution of land)","d":"The popular measure Heracleides promoted, which Dion later repealed."}]},{"i":478,"j":491,"loc":"Life of Dion, chs. 48–58","title":"Victory, Dion's rule, and his murder","summary":"Dion fences off the acropolis, but restores Heracleides' naval command and repeals the land decrees, and Heracleides plots with the Spartan Pharax and the would-be commander Gaesylus before being reconciled under oath. The citadel surrenders and Dion is reunited with his sister, son, and wife Arete. He lives simply, as if before the Academy, yet stays haughty, plans a mixed constitution on Spartan and Cretan lines with Corinthian help, and allows Heracleides' murder, which haunts him. His Athenian comrade Callippus, posing as his informer, plots against him; Dion sees a Fury sweeping his house, and his son dies in a fall. Callippus swears the great oath, then has Dion killed at the Coreia; Callippus, the women, and Hicetas later meet violent ends.","ideas":["Freedom won but a free city hard to govern","Dion's plan to curb unmixed democracy","The Fury apparition and the death of Dion's son","Callippus' perjury and the retribution that follows"],"terms":[{"t":"great oath","d":"An oath sworn in the sanctuary of Demeter and Persephone, wearing the goddess's robe and holding a torch."},{"t":"Coreia","d":"The festival of Persephone (Kore), on which Callippus had Dion murdered."}]},{"i":492,"j":511,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 1–13","title":"Brutus's lineage, character and the gathering conspiracy","summary":"Plutarch sets Marcus Brutus, softened and tempered by philosophy, against his stern ancestor Junius Brutus, and traces his descent, his devotion to his uncle Cato and to the Old Academy, and his laconic Greek letters. Brutus serves under Pompey, his father's killer, for the public good, is spared and honoured by Caesar after Pharsalus, and governs Cisalpine Gaul justly. The rivalry with Cassius over the city praetorship, Cassius's hatred of tyrants, and anonymous writings urging Brutus to act draw him into the plot, which Cassius's friends will join only if Brutus leads. The conspirators recruit Ligarius, Labeo and Decimus Brutus while leaving out Cicero, and the section ends with Porcia wounding her thigh to prove she can be trusted with the secret.","ideas":["Brutus's nature is mild by birth and tempered by philosophic training","Caesar both favours and suspects Brutus, possibly his own son by Servilia","Brutus opposes the rule, Cassius hates the ruler","Porcia's self-inflicted wound as proof of her constancy"],"terms":[{"t":"Old Academy","d":"The school of Plato's early successors, revived by Antiochus of Ascalon, which Brutus preferred to the New and Middle Academy."},{"t":"praetorship of the city","d":"The most prestigious of the praetorships, given by Caesar to Brutus over Cassius's stronger claim."}]},{"i":512,"j":523,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 14–21","title":"The Ides of March and its aftermath","summary":"The conspirators choose the senate meeting in Pompey's portico and wait composedly, Brutus even judging cases as praetor, while alarms multiply: Caesar's delay, an ambiguous remark to Casca, Popilius Laenas's whispered words, and false news of Porcia's death. Caesar is stabbed first by Casca and, seeing Brutus, covers his head and submits. Brutus, who had refused to let Antony be killed, addresses the people from the Capitol, and the senate votes amnesty and provinces. Brutus then lets Antony read the will and hold a public funeral; the crowd is roused, burns Caesar's body in the forum, and tears apart the poet Cinna by mistake, so the conspirators withdraw from Rome.","ideas":["Assassination of Caesar at the foot of Pompey's statue","Brutus's two mistakes: sparing Antony and allowing the public funeral","Antony shows the bloody robe and turns the crowd","The poet Cinna killed in place of his namesake"],"terms":[{"t":"Ides of March","d":"The middle day of March on which the senate met and Caesar was killed."}]},{"i":524,"j":542,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 22–29","title":"From Italy to Asia: Brutus raises his war","summary":"The young Caesar (Octavius) arrives, claims his inheritance and courts the veterans, and Brutus rebukes Cicero for choosing a gentler master rather than freedom. Leaving Italy, Brutus parts from Porcia at Elea, where a painting of Andromache moves her to tears; at Athens he attends philosophers while secretly winning over Macedonia, treasure ships and Pompey's scattered soldiers, captures Caius Antonius, and suffers boulimia in the snow near Epidamnus. Octavius allies with Antony, has Brutus condemned in his absence, and joins the proscriptions in which Cicero dies, so Brutus orders Caius killed. Brutus and Cassius meet at Smyrna with fleets and armies, and Plutarch contrasts Cassius's harshness with Brutus's trusted virtue, which even Antony acknowledged.","ideas":["Brutus prefers war to a shameful peace under a 'kindly' tyrant","Porcia and the Andromache painting","Brutus's ominous Homeric verse and the watchword 'Apollo'","Only Brutus acted from the nobility of the deed, as Antony said"],"terms":[{"t":"boulimia","d":"A ravenous faintness caused by toil in cold and snow, which Plutarch explains through loss of bodily heat."},{"t":"proscription","d":"The triumvirs' published list of men condemned to death, two hundred in number, including Cicero."}]},{"i":543,"j":557,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 30–39","title":"Xanthus, the quarrel at Sardis and the apparition","summary":"Cassius gives Brutus a third of his treasure and treats Rhodes harshly, while Brutus campaigns in Lycia, where the besieged Xanthians, seized by a passion for death, burn their own city despite his efforts to save it; Patara surrenders after he frees its women, and his levy is light compared with Cassius's. Brutus punishes Theodotus, who had counselled Pompey's murder. At Sardis the two generals quarrel behind closed doors until Favonius bursts in quoting Homer, and Brutus later rebukes Cassius for shielding corrupt friends. Before crossing to Europe Brutus sees a phantom that names itself his evil genius and promises to meet him at Philippi; Cassius explains it away on Epicurean grounds, and the armies face each other on the plains of Philippi amid bad omens for Cassius.","ideas":["Mass self-destruction of the Xanthians","Brutus's justice versus Cassius's leniency to friends","The evil genius: 'thou shalt see me at Philippi'","Cassius's Epicurean account of perception and dreams"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (evil genius)","d":"The guardian spirit that appears to Brutus as an ill-omened apparition."},{"t":"Epicurean doctrine","d":"Cassius's view that the senses and imagination deceive and that spirits do not reach human affairs."}]},{"i":558,"j":568,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 40–46","title":"First battle of Philippi and the death of Cassius","summary":"Against Atillius's advice for delay, battle is decided; Cassius confides his misgivings to Messala, and Brutus tells him that, having given his life to his country on the Ides of March, he will accept death if defeated. Brutus's wing charges without the watchword, routs Octavius's legions and sacks his camp, while Antony's men break Cassius's wing. Seeing Titinius surrounded by friendly horsemen and thinking him captured, Cassius has his freedman Pindarus kill him, and Titinius kills himself. Brutus mourns Cassius as 'the last of the Romans', rewards his soldiers, struggles with a divided army and allows the killing of mocking captives, and promises two cities for plunder, which Plutarch calls the one indefensible act of his life.","ideas":["Brutus victorious, Cassius defeated, each unaware of the other's fortune","Cassius's death through a mistaken report","Brutus's changed view of suicide","The promise of Thessalonica and Lacedaemon as booty"],"terms":[]},{"i":569,"j":579,"loc":"Life of Brutus, chs. 47–53","title":"Second battle of Philippi and Brutus's death","summary":"The triumvirs, short of food and having lost a fleet to Brutus's ships, hurry to fight before Brutus hears the news, which reaches him twenty days too late; Plutarch says Heaven was clearing the way for a monarch. The phantom returns, other omens follow, and after a desertion Brutus attacks; his own wing wins, but the other gives way, he is surrounded, and young Cato falls fighting. Lucilius gives himself up as Brutus and is welcomed by Antony as a friend. At night beside a stream Brutus recalls his fallen comrades, then, praising his friends' loyalty and blaming Fortune only for his country's sake, falls on his sword held by Strato; Antony honours the body, and Plutarch weighs accounts of Porcia's death.","ideas":["News of the naval victory fails to reach Brutus in time","Lucilius's impersonation saves Brutus from capture","'Fly, not with our feet but with our hands'","Brutus dies confident in his reputation for virtue"],"terms":[]},{"i":580,"j":587,"loc":"Comparison of Dion and Brutus, chs. 1–5","title":"Dion and Brutus weighed against each other","summary":"Plutarch credits Dion with rising unaided and spending his own wealth for Syracuse, whereas Brutus had Cassius and was driven to war by condemnation. Dionysius was an open tyrant, while Caesar's rule, once accepted, was tyranny in name only and seemed a needed remedy, so Rome missed him. Dion was the better general; but Brutus, with no private grievance, struck at a benefactor for the common liberty, while Dion fought only after being wronged. Overthrowing Caesar demanded far greater courage and choice of trustworthy allies than overthrowing the despised Dionysius, and Brutus's honours were preserved even by his enemies, as Octavius's sparing of his statue at Mediolanum shows.","ideas":["Disinterested versus aggrieved hostility to tyranny","Caesar's monarchy as a gentle physician for Rome's ills","Brutus chose loyal helpers; Dion trusted bad men","Octavius praises the Gauls for keeping Brutus's statue"],"terms":[]},{"i":588,"j":598,"loc":"Life of Artaxerxes, chs. 1–8","title":"Artaxerxes's accession and Cyrus's rebellion","summary":"Plutarch distinguishes Artaxerxes Memor from his grandfather Longimanus and contrasts his mild nature with his brother Cyrus's impetuousness; their mother Parysatis favours Cyrus, but Artaxerxes is made king. At the royal initiation in Pasargadae Cyrus is accused of plotting murder and is saved only by his mother. The king's gentleness and generosity are shown in anecdotes of small gifts and of Teribazus's robe, while Cyrus gathers mercenaries and Spartan support. Stateira's reproaches turn Parysatis against her; the king, urged by Teribazus, meets Cyrus at Cunaxa, and Plutarch blames Clearchus's caution, more than Cyrus's rashness, for the defeat.","ideas":["Rival brothers and a mother's partiality","Persian royal initiation rites at Pasargadae","The king's gracious acceptance of humble gifts","Clearchus's refusal to attack the king's centre"],"terms":[{"t":"Memor (Mnēmōn)","d":"Artaxerxes II's surname, 'the Mindful'."}]},{"i":599,"j":609,"loc":"Life of Artaxerxes, chs. 9–17","title":"The death of Cyrus and its bloody sequel","summary":"Cyrus kills Artagerses, and Plutarch gives Deinon's and Ctesias's differing accounts of Cyrus's death, Ctesias's having Cyrus wounded by Mithridates and finished off by a Caunian. The thirsty king delights in foul water from a Caunian, displays Cyrus's head, and rewards and punishes his men. Wishing to be thought Cyrus's slayer, he gives evasive rewards; the Carian who claims the deed is given to Parysatis and tortured to death, and Mithridates, boasting while drunk, dies by the torture of the boats. Parysatis then wins the eunuch Masabates, who mutilated Cyrus's body, from the king at dice and has him flayed.","ideas":["Conflicting sources on how Cyrus fell","The king's vanity about killing his brother","Parysatis's vengeance for Cyrus","Plutarch's criticism of Ctesias's self-promotion"],"terms":[{"t":"torture of the boats","d":"A Persian execution in which the victim, enclosed between two boats, was left to be eaten by insects and worms."}]},{"i":610,"j":619,"loc":"Life of Artaxerxes, chs. 18–24","title":"Stateira poisoned and Persia's dealings with Greece","summary":"Ctesias tells of the captive Clearchus and his execution, which he gives as Parysatis's motive for killing Stateira; Plutarch doubts it, and recounts the poisoning of Stateira with a half-poisoned bird, the execution of the maid Gigis, and Parysatis's banishment to Babylon. The escape of the Ten Thousand exposes Persian weakness; Agesilaüs invades Asia, but the king's gold stirs war against Sparta, Conon's victory at Cnidus strips Sparta of the sea, and the Peace of Antalcidas hands Asia's Greeks to Persia. The king later abandons Antalcidas, rewards the venal Timagoras, and, reconciled with Parysatis, kills Tissaphernes and marries his daughter Atossa. His Egyptian war fails and his Cadusian expedition falls into famine.","ideas":["Rivalry of queen mother and queen ends in poison","Persian gold, the 'thirty thousand archers', drives Agesilaüs home","The Peace of Antalcidas as Greece's betrayal","Artaxerxes marries his own daughter"],"terms":[{"t":"Peace of Antalcidas","d":"The settlement dictated by the Persian king giving him the Greek cities of Asia."},{"t":"rhyntaces","d":"A small Persian bird, cut with a knife poisoned on one side to kill Stateira."}]},{"i":620,"j":631,"loc":"Life of Artaxerxes, chs. 24–30","title":"Cadusian rescue, succession strife and the king's end","summary":"Teribazus saves the starving army by negotiating separate peaces with the two Cadusian kings, and the king marches on foot like a common soldier, though on returning he kills many nobles out of fear. As his sons contend for the succession, he names Dareius heir; Dareius asks for Cyrus's beloved Aspasia, whom the king makes a priestess instead. Teribazus, wronged over a promised daughter, spurs Dareius to plot the king's murder; the king, forewarned, escapes through a hidden door, Teribazus is killed and Dareius executed. Ochus then drives Ariaspes to suicide and has Arsames murdered, and the aged king dies of grief.","ideas":["Royal hardship disproves that luxury causes cowardice","Aspasia of Phocaea, 'the Wise'","Dareius's failed conspiracy and execution","Ochus clears his way to the throne by murder"],"terms":[{"t":"kitanis","d":"The upright tiara permitted to the designated heir to the Persian throne."}]},{"i":632,"j":650,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 1–9","title":"Aratus's escape, youth and liberation of Sicyon","summary":"Plutarch dedicates the Life to Polycrates, a descendant of Aratus, so his sons may imitate their ancestor. Sicyon, torn by tyrants, sees Aratus's father Cleinias killed by Abantidas; the seven-year-old Aratus is hidden by Soso and sent to Argos, where he grows up an athlete and a hater of tyrants. Learning of a low stretch of wall, he gathers exiles and hired men, deceives Nicocles's spies with a show of revelry, and by night scales the wall despite barking dogs and passing watches. The citizens burn the tyrant's house, Nicocles flees, and no one is killed; to settle the restored exiles and secure the city, Aratus joins Sicyon to the Achaean League, whose concord Plutarch praises.","ideas":["A dedication on praising ancestors worthily","Night escalade of Sicyon's wall","A bloodless liberation","The small Achaean League made great by concord and obedience"],"terms":[{"t":"Achaean League","d":"A federation of small Achaean cities which Sicyon joined under Aratus."}]},{"i":651,"j":661,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 10–17","title":"Aratus's character, Ptolemy's gold and Acrocorinthus","summary":"Plutarch describes Aratus as a statesman bold in secret enterprises but timid in open battle, an unevenness he ascribes to virtue without philosophy. Serving modestly in the League's cavalry, Aratus sails to Egypt through perils to seek money from Ptolemy, wins him with Sicyonian paintings, and uses the gift, with fifteen colleagues, to reconcile exiles and owners; Nealces saves a painting of the tyrant Aristratus by erasing the figure. Antigonus's public flattery provokes Ptolemy's suspicion. As general Aratus plans to free Acrocorinthus, 'the fetters of Greece', which Antigonus had seized by staging his son's wedding to Nicaea and then dashing up to the citadel.","ideas":["Courage by night, timidity by day","Arbitration of the exiles' property claims","The painting of Aristratus spared","Antigonus's trick on Nicaea to win the citadel"],"terms":[{"t":"Acrocorinthus","d":"The citadel of Corinth commanding the Isthmus and so the passage into Peloponnesus."}]},{"i":662,"j":671,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 18–23","title":"The night capture of Acrocorinthus","summary":"A Syrian, Erginus, who stole royal gold, reveals a path to the citadel's lowest wall, and Aratus pledges his own plate and his wife's jewels to pay him. A mistaken identity nearly betrays Technon to Erginus's brother, who is then locked up. On a moonlit night clouds cover the approach; Aratus's men kill the gate guards, climb, and struggle up the cliff, while the three hundred left behind ambush and rout Archelaüs's relief force and then reinforce Aratus. At daybreak the citadel falls; Aratus, exhausted, addresses the Corinthians in the theatre, returns their keys, brings them into the League, and Persaeus later admits the Stoic teaching about the wise general was refuted.","ideas":["Aratus risks his own wealth for the common good","Moon and clouds favour the ascent","The three hundred's chance victory below","Corinth joins the League"],"terms":[]},{"i":672,"j":689,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 24–34","title":"League expansion and wars against the tyrants","summary":"Plutarch calls the capture of Acrocorinthus the last great deed of the Greeks; Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus join, Ptolemy becomes the League's ally, and Aratus is general every other year, seeing the cities as limbs of one body. His attempts on Argos fail, and Plutarch contrasts the fearful tyrant Aristippus, sleeping above a trapdoor, with Aratus's open power; Aratus finally kills Aristippus near Mycenae, though tales of his battle nerves persist. Lydiades resigns his tyranny at Megalopolis but becomes a rival. Aratus avoids battle with the Aetolians, then routs them at Pellene, makes peace with them, attacks the Peiraeus repeatedly, and finally helps buy freedom for Athens.","ideas":["The League as a body whose parts live by union","The tyrant's wretched life versus lawful power","Lydiades, the reformed tyrant never trusted","The vision of the helmeted woman at Pellene"],"terms":[]},{"i":690,"j":704,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 35–42","title":"Cleomenes's war and the call to Antigonus","summary":"Aristomachus gives Argos to the League and becomes general, and the rise of Cleomenes of Sparta begins a long struggle. Aratus loses at Mount Lycaeum but seizes Mantineia; at Megalopolis his refusal to support Lydiades's charge leaves Lydiades to die, and the angered Achaeans cut his funds. Aratus declines the generalship, and Plutarch reports the charge that he should have yielded to the Spartan Cleomenes rather than bring Macedonian garrisons back, citing Polybius and doubting Phylarchus. After Hecatombaeum the League's cities fall away, Aratus escapes a hostile Corinthian assembly, refuses Cleomenes's offers, and at Aegium the Achaeans vote to call in Antigonus and give him Acrocorinthus.","ideas":["Aratus's caution blamed for Lydiades's death","Choosing Macedon over a Heracleid king of Sparta","Aratus slips away from the Corinthians leading his horse","Acrocorinthus surrendered as the price of help"],"terms":[]},{"i":705,"j":722,"loc":"Life of Aratus, chs. 43–54","title":"Under Macedon: Antigonus, Philip and Aratus's death","summary":"Antigonus Doson receives Aratus with special honour, fulfilling an omen of friendship with what he hated; Argos revolts, Cleomenes withdraws, and Acrocorinthus passes to Macedon. Aratus is blamed for the killing of Aristomachus, for honours paid to the king, and for renaming Mantineia Antigoneia. After Sellasia Antigonus entrusts the young Philip to Aratus, whose counsel wins Philip fame, but Philip corrupts the younger Aratus's wife, stirs bloodshed at Messene, and, rebuked about seizing Ithome, grows into a tyrant. He has Aratus slowly poisoned; Aratus dies at Aegium in his seventeenth generalship and, by Delphi's leave, is buried in Sicyon as its founder with yearly rites, and Philip's later downfall is recounted.","ideas":["Aratus as tutor of a king's good conduct","Trust and gratitude, not citadels, as a king's defence","'The wages of royal friendship'","Hero cult of Aratus at Sicyon"],"terms":[{"t":"Soteria","d":"The yearly sacrifice at Sicyon on the day Aratus freed the city."},{"t":"Arateium","d":"Aratus's burial place within Sicyon."}]},{"i":723,"j":735,"loc":"Life of Galba, chs. 1–10","title":"Soldiers' greed and Galba's rise against Nero","summary":"Plutarch opens with a reflection on armies: an empire has nothing more dangerous than soldiery ruled by unreasoning impulse, as the four emperors after Nero show. He blames Nymphidius Sabinus for teaching the praetorians to sell a Caesar's overthrow for a huge promised largess. Galba, rich, nobly connected and mild as governor of Spain, is urged by Vindex's revolt in Gaul and Titus Vinius's counsel to accept power, styling himself General of the Senate and People. After Vindex's defeat and suicide he despairs at Clunia, until Icelus brings news that Nero is dead and Galba proclaimed. Meanwhile Nymphidius grasps at supreme power in Rome, and Verginius Rufus, refusing the throne himself, brings his legions over to Galba.","ideas":["An army must obey; a mercenary soldiery that sells emperors tears the empire apart","Galba accepts power cautiously, refusing the titles Caesar and Emperor at first","Nymphidius claims descent from Caius Caesar and aims at the throne","Verginius Rufus holds that only the senate may choose an emperor"],"terms":[{"t":"praetorian guard","d":"The emperor's household troops at Rome, whose loyalty Nymphidius bought with promises of largess."},{"t":"largess (donative)","d":"A cash gift promised to soldiers on an emperor's accession; Galba's failure to pay it drives the narrative."}]},{"i":736,"j":748,"loc":"Life of Galba, chs. 11–18","title":"Nymphidius falls; Vinius corrupts Galba's rule","summary":"On his march to Rome Galba at first lives simply, but Vinius, a man enslaved to money and women, persuades him to use Nero's riches and comes to direct him. Nymphidius, alarmed at the influence of Vinius and Laco, plans to be proclaimed in the camp, but the tribune Antonius Honoratus rallies the soldiers to Galba, and Nymphidius is cut down. Galba's executions without trial, the slaughter of Nero's marines outside the city, and his stingy recovery of Nero's gifts make him feared and disliked. Vinius sells favours and shields Tigellinus, while Galba's remark that he enrolls soldiers rather than buys them turns the troops, especially the German legions under Flaccus, to open hatred.","ideas":["Vinius's venality brings odium even on Galba's reasonable measures","Honoratus's speech turns the guard against Nymphidius's coup","Galba enters Rome through a massacre of the seamen","Galba refuses to buy his soldiers' loyalty"],"terms":[{"t":"principia","d":"The Roman term for a general's quarters in camp, where Vinius committed his early offence."}]},{"i":749,"j":757,"loc":"Life of Galba, chs. 19–24","title":"Otho, Vitellius, and the adoption of Piso","summary":"Feeling that age and childlessness bring him contempt, Galba resolves to adopt a successor. Plutarch sketches Otho: luxurious friend of Nero, husband of Poppaea, sent to Lusitania, the first governor to join Galba, and a courtier of Vinius and the soldiers. When the German legions overthrow Galba's statues and proclaim Vitellius, Galba suddenly adopts the grave young Piso amid thunder and rain, still without paying the troops. Disappointed Otho, spurred by astrologers and the discarded followers of Tigellinus and Nymphidius, has the soldiers corrupted; on the morning of the murder a priest's warning at sacrifice nearly exposes him before he slips away to the forum.","ideas":["Otho's rise through his marriage to Poppaea and favour with Nero","Vitellius is proclaimed in Germany after the soldiers refuse the oath to Galba","The adoption of Piso is marked by inauspicious storms","Only a few days separate the adoption from the murder"],"terms":[{"t":"optio and tesserarius","d":"Junior army ranks, explained by Plutarch as scout and messenger, held by two of Otho's agents."},{"t":"Germanicus","d":"The title Vitellius accepts from his troops while declining that of Caesar."}]},{"i":758,"j":766,"loc":"Life of Galba, chs. 25–29","title":"Murder of Galba in the Forum","summary":"Otho is hailed emperor by a mere twenty-three soldiers and carried, fearful, into the camp, where resistance melts away. A false report of Otho's death draws Galba out in his litter; in the forum horsemen and troops attack, and only the centurion Sempronius Densus defends him. At Lacus Curtius Galba offers his neck, bidding them strike if it is better for the Roman people; Piso, Vinius and Laco are also killed and their heads brought to Otho, while many claim a share of the deed for reward. The senate swears allegiance to Otho over the headless bodies, and Plutarch's verdict is that Galba, worthy by lineage and repute, failed by giving himself over to Vinius, Laco and freedmen.","ideas":["Sempronius Densus alone is worthy of Rome in defending Galba","Galba's last words offer his death for the people's good","The heads of the slain are sold or mocked","Galba was pitied in death though no one wanted him in power"],"terms":[{"t":"Lacus Curtius","d":"A spot in the Roman forum where Galba's litter was overturned and he was slain."}]},{"i":767,"j":777,"loc":"Life of Otho, chs. 1–6","title":"Otho's mild beginning and the war opens","summary":"Otho begins kindly: he pardons Marius Celsus, confirms consuls and restores exiles' property, and pleases all by forcing Tigellinus to his death, who cuts his own throat. He tolerates being hailed as Nero until the nobles object. A mutiny of soldiers who suspect the senate nearly ends in a massacre of eighty senators at his supper, which he calms with tears and a gift. Abusive letters pass between Otho and Vitellius, omens appear, and the Tiber floods Rome. Otho's soft, insolent praetorians resist their generals, but stung by the Vitellians' taunts they hold Placentia under Spurina, while Plutarch contrasts the outlandish Caecina and the rapacious Valens.","ideas":["Otho's clemency surprises those who feared an avenging spirit","The soldiers' suspicions of the senate erupt at the palace supper","Otho and Vitellius trade reproaches equally true of both","Spurina's defence of Placentia"],"terms":[]},{"i":778,"j":788,"loc":"Life of Otho, chs. 7–14","title":"The campaign and the battle of Bedriacum","summary":"Near Cremona Celsus turns Caecina's ambush, but Paulinus's slowness leaves the victory incomplete and the soldiers accuse their commanders; Otho sends his brother Titianus and Proculus to take control. At Bedriacum Paulinus, Celsus and Gallus advise delay until the Mysian forces arrive, but Otho, anxious and impatient, orders battle; Plutarch reports a rival account that the armies wished to confer and choose an emperor. Otho withdraws to Brixillum, and after setbacks at the Po his men are marched out and fight in disorder; the praetorians flee shamefully. Celsus persuades the officers to seek concord, and the army goes over to Vitellius; Plutarch adds Mestrius Florus's account of a heap of corpses reaching a temple's gable.","ideas":["The council of war at Bedriacum chooses immediate battle against advice","Otho's absence from the field weakens his army","Celsus argues a good man may yield to fortune to spare citizens' lives","Plutarch visits the battlefield and records an eyewitness"],"terms":[{"t":"Rapax and Adiutrix","d":"The legions of Vitellius and Otho, Devourer and Helper, which fought the one regular engagement of the battle."}]},{"i":789,"j":796,"loc":"Life of Otho, chs. 15–18","title":"Otho's noble death","summary":"News of defeat reaches Otho, and his soldiers beg him not to abandon them; one kills himself as a pledge of devotion. Otho replies calmly that a war of Romans against Romans harms the country whichever side wins, and that he can die more honourably than he can reign. He sends away the senators, comforts his nephew Cocceius, quells soldiers threatening the departing senators, sleeps soundly, and at dawn falls on his sword. His troops mourn him, some killing themselves at the pyre; his tomb at Brixillum is modest. Plutarch concludes that Otho lived no better than Nero but died more nobly, and his soldiers, after Verginius Rufus again refuses power, swear to Vitellius.","ideas":["Otho chooses death to end civil war","The extraordinary devotion of Otho's soldiers","A modest tomb and a three months' reign","Verginius Rufus refuses the throne a second time"],"terms":[]}]});